  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 408 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThis Morning: Round 1 of Storms \n\nA cluster of storms, currently ongoing across south-central\nMinnesota this morning, will move southeast through the Coulee \nRegion as we approach daybreak. Members of the 00Z HREF have \ncome into much better agreement as to the track of this MCS \ncompared to earlier 12/18Z runs with it tracking into southeast \nMinnesota and generally along the Mississippi River. There is \nstill some uncertainty as to the strength of this activity by \nthe time it gets to our neck of the woods. While storm strength\nhas persisted on RADAR, entirely elevated instability may \ncontinue to limit surface impacts as seen in recent observations.\n\nAdditionally, the nocturnal jet is expected to be on a downward\ntrend as it is moving into our area. There will be some hail \nrisk with these morning storms given the abundance of elevated \ninstability (1000-2000 MUCAPE and 50 kts of effective shear). \nThe main question will be how long these storms linger with some\nguidance pushing these storms out quickly and others building \nactivity in the immediate vicinity behind it. How quickly these \nstorms vacate the area will play a significant role subsequent \nstorm development detailed below.\n\nThis Afternoon - Tonight: Round 2 of Storms - Strong to Severe Likely\n\nAn upper level shortwave trough over the Canadian Plains is expected \nto continue east southeast through the overnight hours, dropping \ninto the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes Region by late morning/early \nafternoon. This will send a belt of strong mid and upper level winds \nthrough through region with 50-70 kts at 500 mb and 100+ kts at 300 \nmb. At the surface, a cold front is expected to push into SE \nMinnesota and Western Wisconsin, likely by mid-afternoon; with \na warm front potentially in the vicinity of the Mississippi River.\n\nSignificant questions remain as to how the morning storms will \nimpact the airmass for the afternoon storms. The potential \nparameter space is severe-supportive with 50-60 kts of shear \nand 3000-4000+ J/kg SBCAPE; should morning storms clear out \nfairly quickly, giving the airmass time to recover. It's \npossible that the southwesterly winds that are expected to \nmaterialize by late morning could help destabilize us regardless\nof lingering morning convection. Overall, the longer the \nconvection lingers into the morning hours, the more complicated \nthe forecast gets for the afternoon, regarding both timing and \nlocation. \n\nWhere storms can and do develop, an all hazards \nsevere threat looks likely. As mentioned before, shear will be \nplentiful upwards of 60 (maybe even 70) kts with fairly \nsignificant veering in the lower levels and effective SRH values\naround 300 m2/s2 along and ahead of the front. Any storm\ndevelopment will have the potential to produce strong downburst\nwinds with DCAPE values projected to be well over 1000 J/kg per\n19.00 HRRR soundings. Cooling aloft will allow for lapse rates \nto exceed 7 deg C/km, increasing the significant hail threat. \nDew points in the 70s and deep moisture through the profile will\nallow for pWats to climb into the 1.75-2+ inch range tomorrow, \ncreating a brief heavy rain threat. Flooding does not look to be\nof concern as storms should be progressive enough to limit \nflooding problems but these will be efficient rain producers so \nquick bouts of heavy rain will certainly be possible. Initial \nstorms along the front are expected to be supercells with the \nthreat transitioning more to wind/hail as storms grow upscale \ninto more bowing structures as they move to the east/southeast. \n\nTuesday - Beyond: Cooler Temperatures and Generally Dry\n\nOnce the cold front passes and the storms clear out late Monday \nnight, there should be a noticeable difference across the area with \nan overall much cooler airmass expected for mid week. Temperatures\nTuesday and Wednesday will finally drop below average into the \nupper 70s to low 80s with dry conditions expected as a weak \nsurface high pressure moves in behind the front. While a quick \nmoving disturbance may bring some low rain chances to the area \nby late week, expect generally dry conditions and near normal \ntemperatures to round out the next work week.",
